  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened institutional capacity of workers&apos; organizations</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The CPO KHM802 addresses the increased vulnerability of workers in Cambodia&#x2019;s tourism, entertainment, garment, and construction sectors, whose livelihoods have been severely impacted by the COVID-19 pandemic. These sectors, which collectively employ hundreds of thousands, faced mass layoffs, loss of income, and deteriorating working conditions. Tourism and entertainment were among the hardest hit, with over 80,000 entertainment workers losing their jobs. Despite contributing significantly to the economy, many of these workers were excluded from relief measures, prompting calls for equitable access to government support. Similarly, the construction sector, with an estimated 260,000 workers, experienced widespread furloughs and site shutdowns, with no compensation for displaced workers and frequent workplace accidents highlighting a lack of effective OSH enforcement. In the garment sector, home to over 300,000 workers, job losses and pressure to postpone minimum wage negotiations further underscored the fragility of worker protections. The CPO supports trade unions in maximizing their organizing efforts to protect affected workers. Expected results during the biennium include expanded union outreach, improved access to relief measures, and enhanced participation of workers in policy dialogue and decision-making processes. Special emphasis is placed on advocating for improved OSH mechanisms in construction, equitable government support across sectors, and the continuation of wage negotiations in the garment industry. The main beneficiaries are workers in vulnerable sectors, particularly those laid off or facing unsafe conditions, as well as trade unions seeking to represent their interests. The strategy is implemented in collaboration with government institutions and international partners, aligning with broader efforts to promote social dialogue, decent work, and inclusive recovery from the pandemic&#x2019;s impacts.</narrative>
  </description>
